      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While direct confrontation with corporate power was a major catalyst, it wasn't the sole driver of middle-class growth. Government legislation, such as the New Deal programs, also played a pivotal role by establishing social safety nets and regulating industries more broadly. Technological advancements and increased productivity also generated more wealth that the labor movement helped negotiate a fair share of. Therefore, it was a combination of organized worker power, government intervention, and economic shifts that fueled middle-class expansion.",
      "source": "target",
